const walk = (dir: string, relative: string): boolean =>
  listDir(dir).every((name) => {
    const path = join(dir, name);
    const file = posix.join(relative, name);
    let stat: Stats | undefined;
    try {
      stat = lstatSync(path, { throwIfNoEntry: false });
    } catch {
      // A directory listed but not searchable: its entries are skipped like an unlistable one's.
      return true;
    }
    if (stat?.isDirectory()) return SKIPPED_DIRS.has(name) || walk(path, file);
    return !stat?.isFile() || visit(path, file, stat);
  });

findings.body:
**No test checks that treemap cells don't overlap, so the tiling itself is untested.**

`lays out areas in proportion to the values, filling the box` checks three things: each cell's area, the sum of the areas, and that each cell sits inside the box. None of these fails when cells overlap.

Take this mutant: change the last line of `layRow` (chart.utils.ts L355) to `return rest;`. Every row is then laid at the box origin.

- `scale` comes from the original box, so each cell's area stays exact.
- A row's thickness is `rowArea / shortSide`, which is at most the long side, and its cells stack to exactly the short side, so every cell stays in bounds.
- The cells keep their order, so the class-order assertion still passes.

The other tests don't catch it either. The `cells()` helper and the module-map test match `x="…" y="…"` but discard the values, and nothing snapshots positions. So `src/cli` can be drawn on top of `src/core` with CI green.

Moving rows across the box is the one job of the transpose-based `layRow`, and no test fails if it breaks. Since the areas already sum to the box and every cell is in bounds, adding a pairwise no-overlap check proves that the cells tile the box:

```ts
for (const [i, a] of placed.entries()) {
  for (const b of placed.slice(i + 1)) {
    const overlapW = Math.min(a.x + a.w, b.x + b.w) - Math.max(a.x, b.x);
    const overlapH = Math.min(a.y + a.h, b.y + b.h) - Math.max(a.y, b.y);
    expect(Math.min(overlapW, overlapH)).toBeLessThanOrEqual(1e-9);
  }
}
```

